---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PhoneCTA from '../../components/PhoneCTA.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../../config';

const { businessName, siteUrl, guides } = siteConfig;

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": siteUrl },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": `${siteUrl}/guides/` },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Pressure Washing Guides | ${businessName}`}
  description="Practical pressure washing and soft washing guides for Northern Kentucky homeowners, including how mold and algae on vinyl siding are pre-treated and cleaned."
  canonicalURL={`${siteUrl}/guides/`}
  schema={breadcrumbSchema}
>

<section class="hero-section">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Pressure Washing Guides</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">How-to answers for Northern Kentucky homeowners.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="content-section">
  <h2 class="text-brand text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Pressure Washing or Soft Washing? It Depends on the Surface</h2>
  <p>Most exterior cleaning around Northern Kentucky and Cincinnati comes down to one choice: pressure washing or soft washing. People often call both "power washing," and both use water, but they work differently and suit different surfaces. Using the wrong one is how siding gets cracked and decks get gouged.</p>
  <p><strong>Pressure washing</strong> uses high water pressure to strip away dirt, grime, and stains. It's the right tool for hard, durable surfaces like concrete driveways, sidewalks, patios, and brick or stone pavers. A rotating surface cleaner keeps the result even on flat concrete, and oil or rust stains usually need a pre-treatment before washing.</p>
  <p><strong>Soft washing</strong> uses low pressure and a cleaning solution that does most of the work. It's the safer choice for vinyl siding, painted wood, stucco, and roofs, where high pressure can crack panels, force water behind siding, strip paint, or raise and gouge wood grain.</p>
  <p>Our climate is a big reason soft washing matters here. Humid summers in the Ohio River valley, regular rain, and shade from mature trees keep many surfaces damp, and mold, mildew, and algae thrive on that moisture. The green film on north-facing vinyl siding — the side that gets the least sun and stays damp the longest — is usually algae or mildew. Blasting it off with high pressure cleans the surface, but growth left in seams and textured panels tends to come back quickly. A soft wash treats the growth itself, so the siding usually stays clean longer.</p>
  <p>Decks and fences fall in between: a wood cleaner, moderate pressure, and a rinse, with sealing as an option once the wood has dried. The guides below go deeper on specific jobs.</p>

  {guides.map((g) => (
    <div class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-brand text-xl font-bold mb-2"><a href={`/guides/${g.slug}/`}>{g.title}</a></h2>
      <p>{g.summary}</p>
    </div>
  ))}
</div>

<section class="bg-gray-50 border-y-2 border-brand">
  <div class="cta-strip container">
    <p class="cta-heading">Rather Have Someone Else Do It?</p>
    <p class="cta-sub">Call for a free pressure washing estimate.</p>
    <PhoneCTA />
  </div>
</section>

</BaseLayout>
